{
  "A": "Research a portable unsigned-byte MSD radix sort of record indices, replacing quadratic insertion sorting. Use counting, prefix offsets and reusable scatter storage; terminate recursion at the string terminator and use insertion sort only for small partitions. Skip shared prefixes without creating excessive recursion depth. Preserve strcmp ordering, original token bytes, leading zeros and duplicates; never read beyond a token. Support variable token lengths rather than assuming every record has the declared width. Allocate bucket ranges from actual counts, not equal-capacity slices. Prioritize the serial algorithmic improvement and measure complete-program performance under the unchanged harness.",
  "B": "Research a comparison-based alternative: bottom-up mergesort of the complete index array using strcmp. This removes both quadratic insertion costs and dependence on balanced first-character buckets. Reuse one auxiliary index array and preserve the original strings. If the existing build and execution limits already support threading, investigate up to four CPU workers for independent runs and merge work, with a serial cutoff for small inputs. Do not change compiler settings or add unsupported runtime dependencies. Measure whether parallel overhead and extra memory traffic outweigh gains; retain the best correct serial or parallel candidate.",
  "C": "Research buffered input/output and allocation efficiency. Replace repeated fscanf calls with a bounded fread-backed parser and repeated printf calls with buffered newline-delimited output through fwrite. Preserve header meanings, whitespace-separated token behavior, leading zeros and exact output formatting. Handle tokens and headers crossing buffer boundaries, variable token lengths, and a final token without trailing whitespace. Use overflow-safe allocation and indexing. Keep this independently correct candidate close to the original sorting method for attribution, but replace unsafe fixed bucket slices with count/prefix/scatter placement. Make the parser and writer separable so G can combine them with either sorting tree.",
  "G": "Integrate only programs and improvements produced by A, B and C. First identify the best correct standalone candidate using the fixed harness. Then evaluate controlled combinations: A with C's I/O, B with C's I/O, and supported threading only where the child results justify it. Measure every actual integration; gains are not additive, and retaining an unchanged standalone winner is acceptable. Check comparator semantics, token termination, buffer ownership, bucket offsets, allocation arithmetic and exact newline output at integration boundaries. Require the unchanged correctness checks before considering timing. Preserve tests, oracle, compiler, target, permissions and budgets; select the best measured correct program rather than the most elaborate combination.",
  "analysis": "The supplied source targets Problem D, not the other problems in the statement. Its observable behavior is lexicographic strcmp sorting of textual records, followed by one original token per line; numeric sorting is not generally equivalent. Baseline correctness passed four cases, with mean runtime 0.420317 seconds and samples from 0.418750 to 0.422295 seconds. There is no profile or candidate measurement, so bottleneck claims remain hypotheses. Insertion sorting costs O(sum of squared bucket occupancies) comparisons, while equal-sized bucket reservations can overlap or leave uninitialized output indices on uneven distributions. Repeated formatted I/O is another plausible cost, especially after sorting improves. The complementary objectives are radix sorting, robust comparison sorting with conditional CPU parallelism, and buffered I/O. All must remain correct beyond the four observed cases on the fixed four-core ARM64, CPU-only target."
}